1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,720
This is the companion dashboard to the ICA 2026 paper,

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:12,198
Comparing the Effectiveness of AI Proxy Models Across Key Actuarial Projections in Life Insurance.

3
00:00:12,148 --> 00:00:14,558
The question is simple.

4
00:00:14,558 --> 00:00:19,606
Can quick AI models stand in for an insurer's full actuarial models,

5
00:00:19,606 --> 00:00:21,684
which are detailed but slow?

6
00:00:21,684 --> 00:00:24,970
The paper calls these the heavy models.

7
00:00:24,970 --> 00:00:30,330
The study tested this on a real book of life insurance policies, for three jobs:

8
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:34,730
solvency capital, the value of the business already on the books,

9
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:36,761
and the value of new business.

10
00:00:36,761 --> 00:00:39,790
No euro amounts are shown.

11
00:00:39,790 --> 00:00:43,540
Every result is a percentage or a ratio.

12
00:00:44,329 --> 00:00:48,440
The Overview sums up the paper in five numbers.

13
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:53,364
The main finding is what the paper calls the reconciliation paradox.

14
00:00:53,364 --> 00:00:59,910
Take one of the models, Gradient Boosting, estimating each policy's future profits.

15
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:07,204
On a single policy, its errors were large: about 26% of the average policy's value.

16
00:01:07,204 --> 00:01:13,178
Yet when its estimates were added up over a fifth of the portfolio it had never seen,

17
00:01:13,178 --> 00:01:15,568
the total was out by less than 1%.

18
00:01:15,568 --> 00:01:17,784
Its errors fell on both sides,

19
00:01:17,784 --> 00:01:23,029
and were largely unrelated to each other, so they mostly cancelled out.

20
00:01:23,849 --> 00:01:27,741
Any underlined word has a short definition.

21
00:01:27,691 --> 00:01:30,371
Hover over it to read it.

22
00:01:30,371 --> 00:01:33,876
There's a full glossary at the bottom of the page.

23
00:01:34,649 --> 00:01:38,515
You can also type a question into the search box.

24
00:01:38,465 --> 00:01:41,913
Ask, for example, why the errors cancel out,

25
00:01:41,913 --> 00:01:45,205
and it takes you straight to the evidence.

26
00:01:46,049 --> 00:01:50,624
The PVFP tab, short for the present value of future profits,

27
00:01:50,624 --> 00:01:54,284
ranks the models on the insurer's real policies.

28
00:01:54,234 --> 00:01:59,053
Change the way the error is measured, and the order changes.

29
00:01:59,053 --> 00:02:02,984
So the best model depends on what it will be used for.

30
00:02:03,769 --> 00:02:07,854
Hover over any bar to see its exact value.

31
00:02:08,649 --> 00:02:14,757
Many charts can also be shown as a table, or downloaded as a spreadsheet file.

32
00:02:15,529 --> 00:02:21,523
Actual against predicted plots each model's estimates against the full model's answers,

33
00:02:21,523 --> 00:02:24,279
policy by policy and product by product.

34
00:02:24,229 --> 00:02:27,495
It also puts three kinds of model side by side:

35
00:02:27,495 --> 00:02:31,038
a simple model whose workings can be read directly,

36
00:02:31,038 --> 00:02:35,234
a model built from many decision trees, and a neural network.

37
00:02:36,009 --> 00:02:41,214
Portfolio Level follows one model through three levels: single policies,

38
00:02:41,214 --> 00:02:46,202
products, and the total for a fifth of the portfolio it had not seen.

39
00:02:46,152 --> 00:02:50,495
On that total, the error fell below 1%.

40
00:02:51,369 --> 00:02:55,067
The Solvency Two tab turns to capital.

41
00:02:55,017 --> 00:02:59,022
The standard formula sets capital by testing shocks,

42
00:02:59,022 --> 00:03:04,644
such as a fall in share prices, lower interest rates, or higher expenses.

43
00:03:04,644 --> 00:03:09,992
The study trained a separate model for each shock, policy by policy.

44
00:03:09,992 --> 00:03:13,883
Pick a shock, then a way of measuring the error.

45
00:03:14,649 --> 00:03:16,947
Added up over the whole portfolio,

46
00:03:16,947 --> 00:03:22,623
the totals for all eleven shocks were within 0.6% of the full model in the main run,

47
00:03:22,623 --> 00:03:29,056
and within 2.8% when the training was repeated five times from different random starting points.

48
00:03:29,006 --> 00:03:32,087
Taking each fifth of the portfolio on its own,

49
00:03:32,087 --> 00:03:39,522
52 of the 55 totals were within the range you would expect if each policy's error were unrelated to the others.

50
00:03:40,369 --> 00:03:44,235
The value of new business showed the same pattern.

51
00:03:44,185 --> 00:03:50,783
But a model trained once and then left alone was out by 8.3% after just one quarter.

52
00:03:50,783 --> 00:03:56,350
So the new-business models were retrained on each new group of policies sold.

53
00:03:57,169 --> 00:04:02,440
Model Choice shows which kind of model was most accurate for each job.

54
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:06,657
Two things decided it: how smooth the pattern being copied was,

55
00:04:06,657 --> 00:04:10,315
and how many full-model runs there were to learn from.

56
00:04:10,315 --> 00:04:17,050
For the insurer's best estimate of what it owes its policyholders, a fitted formula,

57
00:04:17,050 --> 00:04:22,394
the LSMC polynomial, was out by 1.1% on scenarios it had not seen,

58
00:04:22,394 --> 00:04:25,147
against 12% for Gradient Boosting.

59
00:04:25,147 --> 00:04:30,805
For capital worked out policy by policy, Gradient Boosting did best.

60
00:04:31,609 --> 00:04:36,378
The Proxy-Ability Index asks, before the full-model runs are spent,

61
00:04:36,378 --> 00:04:39,225
which products a quick model can handle.

62
00:04:39,175 --> 00:04:44,703
A small trial of sixteen runs ranked the products by how well they could be copied.

63
00:04:44,703 --> 00:04:49,959
That ranking closely matched the accuracy seen on seventeen new runs,

64
00:04:49,959 --> 00:04:54,987
with a rank correlation of 0.92, where 1 would be a perfect match.

65
00:04:54,987 --> 00:04:57,680
The index explained why.

66
00:04:58,529 --> 00:05:05,307
Explainability asks whether a model that can't be read directly can still be explained to a reviewer.

67
00:05:05,257 --> 00:05:11,372
An explanation tool called SHAP showed correctly which way each factor pushed the result,

68
00:05:11,372 --> 00:05:16,043
and put the factors in the right order, apart from one swapped pair.

69
00:05:16,043 --> 00:05:19,729
But it did not get the size of each effect right.

70
00:05:20,529 --> 00:05:25,233
The Adoption Matrix turns the findings into practical guidance.

71
00:05:25,183 --> 00:05:30,204
For each job, it says which kind of model to use, how to check it,

72
00:05:30,204 --> 00:05:34,693
how often to retrain it, and where it stops being reliable.

73
00:05:34,693 --> 00:05:38,157
The other half says when to run the full model instead:

74
00:05:38,157 --> 00:05:41,559
when assumptions change unevenly across the portfolio,

75
00:05:41,559 --> 00:05:42,632
for new products,

76
00:05:42,632 --> 00:05:47,051
and when markets move outside the conditions the model was trained on.

77
00:05:47,889 --> 00:05:51,166
By Experiment lists every test in the study:

78
00:05:51,166 --> 00:05:55,634
the question it asked, how it was set up, and what it found.

79
00:05:56,409 --> 00:05:59,888
Paper Figures holds all ten figures from the paper,

80
00:05:59,888 --> 00:06:03,368
and the other tabs go into the results behind them.

81
00:06:04,129 --> 00:06:09,013
And you can download all the data behind the dashboard in one click.

82
00:06:09,769 --> 00:06:11,991
If you're new to the dashboard,

83
00:06:11,991 --> 00:06:16,650
the How to Use tab explains each part of the page in plain words.

84
00:06:17,409 --> 00:06:22,448
To repeat: every result here is a percentage or a ratio.

85
00:06:22,398 --> 00:06:27,436
No euro amounts and no individual policy records are published.

86
00:06:27,436 --> 00:06:29,782
Thank you for watching.

